OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026", "title": "A uniformly discrete counterexample to bounded approximation in Lipschitz-free spaces", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/main.pdf", "family": "330", "family_title": "A uniformly discrete counterexample to bounded approximation in Lipschitz-free spaces", "abstract_html": "We construct a countable uniformly discrete metric space whose real Lipschitz-free space has the approximation property but fails the bounded approximation property, answering Kalton's question negatively. The identity can be approximated on every compact set by finite-rank operators, but no uniform bound on their norms is possible.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a countable, unbounded, nonproper pointed metric space \\((M,d,o)\\) such that \\( d(x,y)\\ge1\\qquad(x\\ne y) \\) and \\(\\mathcal F(M)\\) has AP but fails \\(\\lambda\\)-BAP for every finite \\(\\lambda\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All Banach spaces and linear operators are over the real scalars.</p><p class=\"li\">For a pointed metric space \\((M,d,o)\\), let \\(\\operatorname{Lip}_0(M)\\) be the Banach space of real Lipschitz functions vanishing at \\(o\\), with the Lipschitz constant as norm. Its Lipschitz-free space is\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F(M)=\\overline{\\operatorname{span}}\\{\\delta_x:x\\in M\\}\n  \\subseteq \\operatorname{Lip}_0(M)^*,\n  \\qquad \\delta_x(f)=f(x).\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(\\delta_o=0\\) and \\(\\|\\delta_x-\\delta_y\\|=d(x,y)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A Banach space \\(X\\) has the approximation property, abbreviated AP, if, for every norm-compact \\(C\\subseteq X\\) and every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there is a bounded finite-rank linear operator \\(T:X\\to X\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\sup_{u\\in C}\\|Tu-u\\|&lt;\\varepsilon.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\lambda\\ge1\\), the space has \\(\\lambda\\)-BAP if these operators can always be chosen with \\(\\|T\\|\\le\\lambda\\). It has BAP if it has \\(\\lambda\\)-BAP for some finite \\(\\lambda\\), and MAP if it has \\(1\\)-BAP.</p><p class=\"li\">A metric space is proper if its closed bounded balls are compact.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a countable, unbounded, nonproper pointed metric space \\((M,d,o)\\) such that\n\\[\nd(x,y)\\ge1\\qquad(x\\ne y)\n\\]\nand \\(\\mathcal F(M)\\) has AP but fails \\(\\lambda\\)-BAP for every finite \\(\\lambda\\ge1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For every integer \\(p\\ge1\\), construct the block \\((M_p,d_p,o)\\) and its finite anchor set \\(A_p\\) by Proposition 4. Proposition 7 gives\n\\[\n\\max_{a\\in A_p}\\|T_0\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|\\ge\\frac12\n\\]\nfor every bounded finite-rank operator \\(T_0:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\to\\mathcal F(M_p)\\) with \\(\\|T_0\\|\\le p\\).</p><p>These blocks satisfy all assumptions of Proposition 2. Form their pointed metric wedge:\n\\[\nM=\\{o\\}\\cup\\bigsqcup_{p\\ge1}(M_p\\setminus\\{o\\}),\n\\]\nretaining the metric within each block and using\n\\[\nd(x,y)=d_p(x,o)+d_q(y,o)\n\\]\nbetween nonbasepoint points in distinct blocks.</p><p>Proposition 2 proves that \\(M\\) is countable, unbounded, nonproper, and separated by at least one, and that \\(\\mathcal F(M)\\) has AP but fails every finite BAP bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "the unnumbered nonlinear consequence stated in the Introduction and proved in Section 2.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The separable Banach space \\(X=\\mathcal F(M)\\) has AP but is not approximable.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\) be the metric wedge constructed in the proof of Theorem 1, and let \\(X=\\mathcal F(M)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A separable Banach space \\(X\\) is approximable if there is an equi-uniformly continuous sequence of maps \\(\\psi_n:X\\to X\\), each with relatively compact range, such that \\(\\psi_n(x)\\to x\\) for every \\(x\\in X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Equi-uniform continuity means that, for every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there is \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) such that\n  \\[\n  \\|x-y\\|&lt;\\delta\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\|\\psi_n(x)-\\psi_n(y)\\|&lt;\\varepsilon\n  \\]\n  for every \\(n\\) and every \\(x,y\\in X\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The separable Banach space \\(X=\\mathcal F(M)\\) has AP but is not approximable.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(X=\\mathcal F(M)\\) for the wedge in Theorem 1. It is separable because \\(M\\) is countable, and\n\\[\n\\{\\delta_x:x\\in M\\}\n\\]\nis \\(1\\)-separated in \\(X\\). Extend this set to a maximal \\(1\\)-separated set \\(N_X\\subseteq X\\). Every point of \\(X\\) is then within distance one of \\(N_X\\), so \\(N_X\\) is a net: a separated set within a fixed distance of every point of \\(X\\).</p><p>Suppose that \\(X\\) were approximable. Kalton’s characterization by nets, cited in the paper as [13, Theorem 4.4 and p. 1260], would give one Lipschitz bound \\(\\Lambda\\ge1\\) for maps\n\\[\ng:N_X\\to N_X\n\\]\nwith relatively compact range that approximate any prescribed finite subset as closely as desired.</p><p>Separation makes each such range finite and turns approximation within distance less than one into exact fixing. For a finite \\(F\\subseteq M\\) containing \\(o\\), choose \\(g\\) fixing every \\(\\delta_x\\) with \\(x\\in F\\). The restriction\n\\[\nx\\longmapsto g(\\delta_x)\n\\]\nis then a pointed \\(\\Lambda\\)-Lipschitz map from \\(M\\) into \\(X\\).</p><p>The Banach-valued linearization property of free spaces, cited in the paper as [12, Lemma 3.2], gives a finite-rank operator\n\\[\nT_F:X\\to X,\n\\qquad\n\\|T_F\\|\\le\\Lambda,\n\\qquad\nT_F\\delta_x=g(\\delta_x)\\quad(x\\in M).\n\\]\nIts rank is finite because the range of \\(g\\) is finite.</p><p>To approximate a compact set, approximate a finite net in it by finite evaluation combinations and put all their supports in \\(F\\). The operator \\(T_F\\) fixes those combinations, and\n\\[\n\\|T_F-\\operatorname{Id}\\|\\le\\Lambda+1\n\\]\ncontrols the remaining errors. Thus these operators give BAP, contrary to Theorem 1.</p><p>Hence \\(X\\) is not approximable. Its AP is already supplied by Theorem 1.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 1.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For each integer \\(p\\ge1\\), real \\(\\ell_1\\) has an equivalent norm \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_p\\) such that \\( \\frac12\\|c\\|_1\\le \\|c\\|_p\\le B_p\\|c\\|_1, \\) and \\((\\ell_1,\\|\\cdot\\|_p)\\) has \\(2B_p\\)-BAP but fails \\(p\\)-BAP.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For each integer \\(p\\ge1\\), put\n  \\[\n  B_p=300p^2+150p+5.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The symbols AP, \\(p\\)-BAP, and MAP have the meanings specified in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each integer \\(p\\ge1\\), real \\(\\ell_1\\) has an equivalent norm \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_p\\) such that\n\\[\n\\frac12\\|c\\|_1\\le \\|c\\|_p\\le B_p\\|c\\|_1,\n\\]\nand \\((\\ell_1,\\|\\cdot\\|_p)\\) has \\(2B_p\\)-BAP but fails \\(p\\)-BAP. For \\(p=1\\), this norm fails MAP.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(p\\ge1\\). Propositions 4 and 7 provide a countably infinite block \\(M_p\\) with separation at least one, diameter at most\n\\[\nB_p=300p^2+150p+5,\n\\]\nand the finite-anchor obstruction of original Theorem 1.2.</p><p>An enumeration of \\(M_p\\setminus\\{o\\}\\) identifies its coefficient space with real \\(\\ell_1\\). Apply Proposition 1 with \\(B=B_p\\), denote its coefficient map by \\(J_p\\), and define\n\\[\n\\|c\\|_p=\\|J_pc\\|_{\\mathcal F(M_p)}.\n\\]\nEquation (2.1) gives\n\\[\n\\frac12\\|c\\|_1\\le\\|c\\|_p\\le B_p\\|c\\|_1,\n\\]\nso this is an equivalent norm. For this norm, \\(J_p\\) is a surjective isometry.</p><p>Conjugation by \\(J_p\\) preserves operator norms, finite rank, and approximation errors on corresponding compact sets. It therefore transfers \\(2B_p\\)-BAP from \\(\\mathcal F(M_p)\\) to \\((\\ell_1,\\|\\cdot\\|_p)\\), with\n\\[\n2B_p=600p^2+300p+10.\n\\]</p><p>If the new norm had \\(p\\)-BAP, the same conjugation would give \\(p\\)-BAP on \\(\\mathcal F(M_p)\\). Applying it with accuracy \\(1/2\\) to the finite set\n\\[\n\\{\\delta_a:a\\in A_p\\}\n\\]\nwould contradict Proposition 7. For \\(p=1\\), this is precisely failure of MAP.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 2.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The map \\( J:\\ell_1(N\\setminus\\{o\\})\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F(N), \\qquad Jc=\\sum_{x\\ne o}c_x\\delta_x, \\) is a linear isomorphism onto \\(\\mathcal F(N)\\) and satisfies \\( \\frac12\\|c\\|_1\\le\\|Jc\\|\\le B\\|c\\|_1. \\) If …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((N,d,o)\\) be a countable pointed metric space such that\n  \\[\n  d(x,y)\\ge1\\quad(x\\ne y),\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{diam}N\\le B&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map\n\\[\nJ:\\ell_1(N\\setminus\\{o\\})\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F(N),\n\\qquad\nJc=\\sum_{x\\ne o}c_x\\delta_x,\n\\]\nis a linear isomorphism onto \\(\\mathcal F(N)\\) and satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac12\\|c\\|_1\\le\\|Jc\\|\\le B\\|c\\|_1. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nIf \\(N\\ne\\{o\\}\\), then \\(\\mathcal F(N)\\) has \\(2B\\)-BAP. If \\(N=\\{o\\}\\), both spaces are zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>First let \\(c\\) be finitely supported. Since \\(\\|\\delta_x\\|=d(x,o)\\le B\\), the triangle inequality gives the upper estimate in (2.1). For the lower estimate, define \\(f_c:N\\to\\mathbb R\\) to be \\(\\operatorname{sign}(c_x)/2\\) at each nonbasepoint in the support of \\(c\\), and zero elsewhere. This function vanishes at \\(o\\). Its values differ by at most one, so the separation assumption makes it \\(1\\)-Lipschitz. Testing the evaluation combination against \\(f_c\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|Jc\\|\\ge(Jc)(f_c)=\\frac12\\sum_{x\\ne o}|c_x|.\n\\]</p><p>The upper estimate makes the series defining \\(Jc\\) converge in \\(\\mathcal F(N)\\) for every \\(c\\in\\ell_1(N\\setminus\\{o\\})\\). Both estimates pass to this extension by approximation with finitely supported families. In particular, \\(J\\) is injective and its inverse on its range has norm at most two.</p><p>The range is closed: if \\(Jc_m\\) converges, then\n\\[\n\\|c_m-c_j\\|_1\\le2\\|Jc_m-Jc_j\\|\n\\]\nmakes \\((c_m)\\) Cauchy, and continuity of \\(J\\) identifies the limit of \\((Jc_m)\\) as the image of the limit of \\((c_m)\\). The range also contains every evaluation \\(\\delta_x\\), whose span is dense in \\(\\mathcal F(N)\\). It is therefore all of \\(\\mathcal F(N)\\).</p><p>Suppose now that \\(N\\ne\\{o\\}\\); then \\(B\\ge1\\). Choose increasing finite sets \\(E_m\\) exhausting \\(N\\setminus\\{o\\}\\), and let \\(q_m\\) be the associated coordinate projections on \\(\\ell_1(N\\setminus\\{o\\})\\). The operators\n\\[\nS_m=Jq_mJ^{-1}:\\mathcal F(N)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F(N)\n\\]\nhave finite rank and norm at most \\(B\\cdot1\\cdot2=2B\\). Since \\(q_mc\\to c\\) in \\(\\ell_1\\), they satisfy \\(S_mu\\to u\\) for every \\(u\\in\\mathcal F(N)\\).</p><p>This convergence is uniform on compact sets. Indeed, for a nonempty compact \\(C\\subseteq\\mathcal F(N)\\) and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), choose a finite \\(\\varepsilon/[3(2B+1)]\\)-net in \\(C\\). For all sufficiently large \\(m\\), the error \\(\\|S_mu-u\\|\\) is less than \\(\\varepsilon/3\\) at every point of the net. Since\n\\[\n\\|S_m-\\operatorname{Id}\\|\\le2B+1,\n\\]\nthe error at any point of \\(C\\) is then less than \\(2\\varepsilon/3\\), hence less than \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>The empty compact set imposes no condition. If \\(N\\) is finite, the sequence of coordinate projections is eventually the identity. This proves \\(2B\\)-BAP. The assertion for \\(N=\\{o\\}\\) is immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Sections 2.2–2.3, the metric wedge reduction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "These rules define a countable, uniformly discrete, unbounded, nonproper metric space \\((M,d,o)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For every integer \\(p\\ge1\\), suppose that \\((M_p,d_p,o)\\) is a bounded countably infinite pointed metric space and that \\(A_p\\subseteq M_p\\) is finite and contains \\(o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that\n  \\[\n  d_p(x,y)\\ge1\\quad(x\\ne y),\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{diam}M_p\\le B_p:=300p^2+150p+5.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that every bounded finite-rank linear operator\n  \\[\n  T_0:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F(M_p),\n  \\qquad \\|T_0\\|\\le p,\n  \\]\n  satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\max_{a\\in A_p}\\|T_0\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|\\ge\\frac12.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Take disjoint copies of the \\(M_p\\) and identify only their basepoints:\n  \\[\n  M=\\{o\\}\\cup\\bigsqcup_{p\\ge1}(M_p\\setminus\\{o\\}).\n  \\]\n  Retain \\(d_p\\) within each block, and set\n  \\[\n  d(x,y)=d_p(x,o)+d_q(y,o)\n  \\quad\n  \\bigl(x\\in M_p\\setminus\\{o\\},\\\n  y\\in M_q\\setminus\\{o\\},\\ p\\ne q\\bigr). \\tag{2.2}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These rules define a countable, uniformly discrete, unbounded, nonproper metric space \\((M,d,o)\\). Each block has a pointed \\(1\\)-Lipschitz retraction from \\(M\\), inducing contractions\n\\[\nI_p:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F(M),\n\\qquad\nP_p:\\mathcal F(M)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F(M_p),\n\\]\nwith \\(P_pI_p=\\operatorname{Id}_{\\mathcal F(M_p)}\\), and \\(I_p\\) is an isometry.</p><p>Moreover, \\(\\mathcal F(M)\\) has AP but fails \\(\\lambda\\)-BAP for every finite \\(\\lambda\\ge1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The rules define a metric. Symmetry and positivity are immediate. For the triangle inequality, if the endpoints lie in one block, a detour through a point \\(z\\) in another block has length\n\\[\nd_p(x,o)+2d_q(z,o)+d_p(y,o)\\ge d_p(x,y).\n\\]\nIf the endpoints lie in different blocks and the third point lies in one of those blocks, that block’s triangle inequality gives the required bound. A third point in a third block adds twice its distance to \\(o\\), and a third point equal to \\(o\\) gives equality. The remaining case is the triangle inequality within a single block.</p><p>The space \\(M\\) is countable. Within a block, distinct points remain at distance at least one; in distinct blocks, nonbasepoint points are at distance at least two.</p><p>It is also unbounded. Suppose that \\(M\\) had finite diameter \\(C_0\\). Then \\(C_0\\ge1\\), and every \\(M_p\\) would have diameter at most \\(C_0\\). Proposition 1 would give \\(2C_0\\)-BAP on every \\(\\mathcal F(M_p)\\). Choose an integer\n\\[\np\\ge\\max\\{1,2C_0\\}.\n\\]\nApplying that property to \\(\\{\\delta_a:a\\in A_p\\}\\) with accuracy \\(1/2\\) would produce a finite-rank operator of norm at most \\(2C_0\\le p\\) whose error is less than \\(1/2\\) at every anchor, contrary to the assumed obstruction.</p><p>The closed ball about \\(o\\) of radius \\(B_1\\) contains the countably infinite \\(1\\)-separated set \\(M_1\\). If this ball were compact, its cover by open balls of radius \\(1/3\\) would admit a finite subcover. Each such ball contains at most one point of \\(M_1\\), which is impossible. Thus \\(M\\) is nonproper.</p><p>For each \\(p\\), let \\(i_p:M_p\\to M\\) be inclusion and let \\(r_p:M\\to M_p\\) fix \\(M_p\\) and send all other blocks to \\(o\\). Both are pointed \\(1\\)-Lipschitz maps. Inclusion preserves distances. For the retraction, the only case needing an inequality has \\(x\\in M_p\\) and \\(y\\in M_q\\setminus\\{o\\}\\), where \\(q\\ne p\\); then\n\\[\nd_p(r_p(x),r_p(y))=d_p(x,o)\\le d(x,y).\n\\]\nIf both points are outside \\(M_p\\), their images coincide, and if both are in \\(M_p\\), their distance is preserved.</p><p>For a pointed \\(1\\)-Lipschitz map \\(u:N\\to N'\\), define on finite evaluation combinations\n\\[\n\\widehat u\\left(\\sum_xc_x\\delta_x\\right)\n=\\sum_xc_x\\delta_{u(x)}.\n\\]\nPullback of pointed scalar tests gives\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\sum_xc_x\\delta_{u(x)}\\right\\|\n=\n\\sup_{\\substack{f\\in\\operatorname{Lip}_0(N')\\\\\n\\|f\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}\\le1}}\n\\left|\\sum_xc_x(f\\circ u)(x)\\right|\n\\le\n\\left\\|\\sum_xc_x\\delta_x\\right\\|.\n\\]\nThis inequality makes the definition independent of the representation of the evaluation combination, and gives a unique continuous linear extension\n\\[\n\\widehat u:\\mathcal F(N)\\to\\mathcal F(N')\n\\]\nof norm at most one.</p><p>Applying this to \\(i_p\\) and \\(r_p\\) gives the contractions \\(I_p\\) and \\(P_p\\). Their composite \\(P_pI_p\\) fixes every evaluation, so it is the identity on \\(\\mathcal F(M_p)\\). Consequently, for \\(v\\in\\mathcal F(M_p)\\),\n\\[\n\\|v\\|=\\|P_pI_pv\\|\\le\\|I_pv\\|\\le\\|v\\|,\n\\]\nand \\(I_p\\) is an isometry.</p><p>Suppose that \\(\\mathcal F(M)\\) had \\(\\lambda\\)-BAP for some finite \\(\\lambda\\ge1\\). Choose an integer \\(p\\ge\\lambda\\) and apply that property with accuracy \\(1/2\\) to the finite set\n\\[\n\\{I_p\\delta_a:a\\in A_p\\}.\n\\]\nLet \\(U\\) be the resulting operator. The compression \\(P_pUI_p\\) has finite rank and norm at most \\(\\lambda\\le p\\), while\n\\[\n\\|P_pUI_p\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|\n\\le\n\\|UI_p\\delta_a-I_p\\delta_a\\|\n&lt;\\frac12\n\\qquad(a\\in A_p).\n\\]\nThis contradicts the block obstruction.</p><p>To prove AP, for \\(N\\ge1\\) define \\(r_{\\le N}:M\\to M\\) to retain the first \\(N\\) blocks and send every other block to \\(o\\). It is pointed and \\(1\\)-Lipschitz: distances in the retained part are unchanged, distances in the collapsed part become zero, and (2.2) bounds the image distance for a retained point and a collapsed point. Its linearization is a contractive projection\n\\[\nR_N=\\sum_{p=1}^NI_pP_p. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nThe equality follows on each evaluation \\(\\delta_x\\): the sum retains it when \\(x\\) is in one of the first \\(N\\) blocks and sends it to zero otherwise. The same check gives \\(R_N^2=R_N\\), and continuity gives both identities on \\(\\mathcal F(M)\\).</p><p>Every finite linear combination of evaluations is fixed by \\(R_N\\) for all sufficiently large \\(N\\). These combinations are dense, and\n\\[\n\\|\\operatorname{Id}-R_N\\|\\le2.\n\\]\nThus \\(R_Nu\\to u\\) for every \\(u\\in\\mathcal F(M)\\): if \\(v\\) is a finite evaluation combination fixed by \\(R_N\\), then\n\\[\n\\|R_Nu-u\\|\\le2\\|u-v\\|.\n\\]\nThe finite-net argument in Proposition 1 makes this convergence uniform on every norm-compact set.</p><p>Fix a nonempty norm-compact \\(C\\subseteq\\mathcal F(M)\\) and \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\). Choose \\(N\\ge1\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sup_{u\\in C}\\|R_Nu-u\\|&lt;\\varepsilon/2.\n\\]\nFor each \\(1\\le p\\le N\\), the image \\(P_p(C)\\) is compact. By Proposition 1, choose a finite-rank operator \\(T_p:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\to\\mathcal F(M_p)\\) with \\(\\|T_p\\|\\le2B_p\\) and\n\\[\n\\sup_{u\\in C}\\|T_pP_pu-P_pu\\|&lt;\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2N}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nT=\\sum_{p=1}^NI_pT_pP_p\n\\]\nhas finite rank and is bounded, with\n\\[\n\\|T\\|\\le\\sum_{p=1}^N2B_p&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nFor every \\(u\\in C\\), (2.3) gives\n\\[\n\\|Tu-u\\|\n\\le\n\\|R_Nu-u\\|\n+\\sum_{p=1}^N\\|T_pP_pu-P_pu\\|\n&lt;\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nThe combined norm may depend on \\(C\\) and \\(\\varepsilon\\), as AP permits.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a deterministic sequence of nonempty finite \\(\\Delta\\)-regular multigraphs \\(G_m\\), with vertices colored by \\(\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}\\), such that, for every fixed integer \\(r\\ge0\\) and every possible induced rooted …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Delta\\ge4\\) be an even integer, and let \\(k\\) be a positive integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Multiple edges are distinct edge instances. Each edge instance has two oriented incidences \\(e,\\bar e\\), exchanged by reversal, even when the edge is a loop. A loop contributes two to the degree.</p><p class=\"li\">Graph distance uses unit edge lengths.</p><p class=\"li\">A rooted radius-\\(r\\) ball is the induced multigraph on vertices at graph distance at most \\(r\\) from the root. Its isomorphism type retains the colors, loops, and edge multiplicities.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a deterministic sequence of nonempty finite \\(\\Delta\\)-regular multigraphs \\(G_m\\), with vertices colored by \\(\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}\\), such that, for every fixed integer \\(r\\ge0\\) and every possible induced rooted colored radius-\\(r\\) type \\(\\tau\\), the fraction of vertices of \\(G_m\\) whose ball has type \\(\\tau\\) converges to the probability of \\(\\tau\\) for the \\(\\Delta\\)-regular tree with independent uniform vertex colors.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a vertex set of size \\(N\\), choose \\(\\Delta/2\\) independent uniform permutations\n\\[\n\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_{\\Delta/2}.\n\\]\nFor each pair \\((a,v)\\), create a distinct undirected edge instance joining \\(v\\) to \\(\\sigma_a(v)\\). For each \\(a\\), every vertex has one incidence directed to its image and one directed to its preimage. A fixed point gives a loop with these two incidences, and a permutation \\(2\\)-cycle gives two parallel edge instances. The resulting multigraph is exactly \\(\\Delta\\)-regular. Assign independent uniform colors in \\(\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}\\) to its vertices, independently of the permutations.</p><p>Fix \\(r\\ge0\\). Explore from a prescribed root in breadth-first order, revealing every permutation image and inverse image at each vertex discovered at distance at most \\(r\\). Record newly discovered vertices at distance \\(r+1\\) without expanding them. This extra layer reveals every edge of the induced radius-\\(r\\) ball, including edges between boundary vertices. The number of queries and discovered vertices is bounded by a constant \\(C=C(\\Delta,r)\\), independent of \\(N\\).</p><p>Conditional on all revelations so far, the unrevealed part of each permutation is a uniform bijection between its unused domains and ranges. A query already exposed reuses its edge instance. A genuinely unexposed image or inverse image is uniform among at least \\(N-C\\) unused possibilities, at most \\(C\\) of which are previously discovered vertices. Its conditional probability of such a collision is at most \\(C/(N-C)\\) when \\(N&gt;C\\).</p><p>Before a collision, each fresh query adds one vertex and one edge to the exploration tree; reused queries add nothing. In particular, a loop or a second parallel edge is detected when its fresh query hits a known vertex. A union bound over the queries makes the probability of any collision at most\n\\[\n\\frac{C^2}{N-C}=O_{\\Delta,r}(N^{-1}).\n\\]\nOutside this event, the induced radius-\\(r\\) ball is the regular-tree ball.</p><p>For two distinct prescribed roots, put both in the initially discovered set and perform the two explorations together. With a larger constant \\(C(\\Delta,r)\\), the same bound applies, counting a hit on either exploration as a collision. With probability tending to one, the two induced radius-\\(r\\) balls are disjoint tree balls. These events concern only the permutations, so the colors on all their distinct vertices remain independent and uniform.</p><p>For a rooted radius-\\(r\\) colored type \\(\\tau\\), let \\(I_v\\) indicate that the ball at \\(v\\) has type \\(\\tau\\), and put\n\\[\nX_{\\tau,N}=\\frac1N\\sum_vI_v,\n\\qquad\nq_\\tau=\\mathbb P(\\text{the colored tree ball has type }\\tau).\n\\]\nThe one-root exploration gives \\(\\mathbb EX_{\\tau,N}\\to q_\\tau\\). By exchangeability and the two-root exploration,\n\\[\n\\mathbb EX_{\\tau,N}^2\n=\n\\frac1N\\mathbb EI_1+\n\\left(1-\\frac1N\\right)\\mathbb E(I_1I_2)\n\\longrightarrow q_\\tau^2.\n\\]\nThis also covers non-tree types, for which \\(q_\\tau=0\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(X_{\\tau,N})\\to0,\n\\]\nand each frequency converges in probability to \\(q_\\tau\\).</p><p>For each \\(m\\ge1\\), there are only finitely many possible colored types at radii at most \\(m\\): bounded degree bounds the number of vertices and edge instances in each ball, and the color set is finite. Convergence in probability and a finite union bound show that, for all sufficiently large \\(N\\), with positive probability every one of these frequencies differs from its tree probability by less than \\(1/m\\).</p><p>Choose such a realization with \\(N=N_m\\) strictly increasing in \\(m\\). For each fixed radius and type, the error then tends to zero, which is the asserted deterministic convergence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Section 3.2 and Lemma 3.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The construction has the following properties.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer \\(p\\ge1\\), and set\n  \\[\n  L=10p,\\qquad R=3L=30p=:R_p,\\qquad K=10p(R+1),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  D=K+2L+5,\\qquad B=4R+D,\\qquad V=1+4pB. \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]\n  Then\n  \\[\n  B=300p^2+150p+5=B_p,\\qquad\n  R&gt;2L,\\qquad\n  \\frac{2pR+1}{K}\\le\\frac15,\n  \\qquad\n  K+2L+1=D-4&lt;D.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose an even integer \\(\\Delta\\ge4\\) such that \\(V/\\Delta&lt;1/20\\), and set\n  \\[\n  Q=\\Delta^{2L+1},\n  \\qquad\n  H=\\Delta^{D+1}.\n  \\]\n  Choose an integer \\(k&gt;2\\Delta QH\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a deterministic sequence \\((G_m)_{m\\ge1}\\) supplied by Proposition 3 for this \\(\\Delta\\) and \\(k\\), and take disjoint copies of its graphs. Their vertices are called source vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(x\\in G_m\\), define \\(h(x)\\in\\{0,\\ldots,D\\}^k\\) by\n  \\[\n  h_i(x)=\n  \\min\\left\\{\n  D,\\operatorname{dist}_{G_m}\n  \\bigl(x,\\{\\text{vertices of color }i\\}\\bigr)\n  \\right\\}\n  \\quad(1\\le i\\le k).\n  \\]\n  The distance is \\(+\\infty\\) if the component of \\(x\\) contains no vertex of color \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H\\subseteq\\{0,\\ldots,D\\}^k\\) be the set of labels realized by all source vertices. For each \\(v\\in\\mathcal H\\), add one anchor \\(a_v\\) with label \\(h(a_v)=v\\). Put\n  \\[\n  A_p=\\{a_v:v\\in\\mathcal H\\},\n  \\qquad\n  a(x)=a_{h(x)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Retain each source edge with length one. Join each source vertex \\(x\\) to \\(a(x)\\) by an edge of length \\(R\\). Join distinct anchors \\(a_v,a_w\\) by an edge of length\n  \\[\n  2R+\\|v-w\\|_\\infty.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M_p\\) consist of all source vertices and anchors. Edge interiors are not points of \\(M_p\\). Let \\(d_p(u,v)\\) be the infimum of lengths of finite paths joining \\(u\\) to \\(v\\), allowing the zero-edge path when \\(u=v\\). Choose any anchor \\(o\\) as basepoint.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The construction has the following properties.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\((M_p,d_p,o)\\) is a countably infinite pointed metric space with separation at least one and diameter at most \\(B=B_p\\). The anchor set \\(A_p\\) is finite and contains \\(o\\). Each source edge has endpoints at \\(d_p\\)-distance at most one.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The label map \\(h:M_p\\to\\{0,\\ldots,D\\}^k\\) is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz for the sup norm. If a source vertex \\(x\\) has color \\(i\\), then \\(h_i(x)=0\\), and \\(h(a(x))=h(x)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. For every source vertex \\(x\\) and every anchor \\(a\\),\n   \\[\n   d_p(x,a)\\ge R=R_p,\n   \\qquad\n   d_p(x,a(x))=R.\n   \\]\n   In particular, \\(d_p(x,o)\\ge R_p=30p\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Every closed \\(d_p\\)-ball of radius \\(2L\\) has at most \\(Q\\) points. For every \\(t\\in M_p\\), at most \\(H\\) coordinates of \\(h(t)\\) are strictly below \\(D\\).</p><p>All parameters, the entire graph sequence, the metric block, and its anchors are fixed before any operator is considered.</p>", "proof": "<p>There are at most \\((D+1)^k\\) possible labels, so \\(A_p\\) is finite. The disjoint union of the nonempty finite graphs is countably infinite, and adjoining finitely many anchors preserves that cardinality. The added edges connect all vertices.</p><p>Every edge length is a positive integer: this is clear for source and attachment edges, and the sup distance between two integer labels is an integer. For distinct endpoints, the lengths of connecting finite paths therefore form a nonempty subset of the positive integers, which has a least element. Thus the infimum defining \\(d_p\\) is attained. Symmetry and the triangle inequality follow from reversal and concatenation of paths, and distinct points have distance at least one. This also justifies using a shortest path despite the possible infinite degrees of anchors.</p><p>A source edge itself gives distance at most one between its endpoints; for a loop the distance is zero.</p><p>The sup distance between any two labels is at most \\(D\\). Two source vertices with different anchors can be joined by their two attachment edges and one anchor edge, with total length at most\n\\[\nR+(2R+D)+R=4R+D=B.\n\\]\nIf their anchors agree, the route through that single anchor has length \\(2R\\). A source vertex and an anchor have distance at most \\(3R+D\\), and two anchors have distance at most \\(2R+D\\), with the coincident-anchor cases shorter. These bounds prove the diameter assertion.</p><p>Any path from a source vertex to an anchor must enter the anchor set for the first time along an attachment edge of length \\(R\\). Hence \\(d_p(x,a)\\ge R\\) for every anchor \\(a\\). The edge from \\(x\\) to \\(a(x)\\) realizes length \\(R\\).</p><p>Across a source edge, the distances to a fixed color class differ by at most one whenever that color is present in the component: append the edge to a path realizing either distance. Capping at \\(D\\) preserves this bound. If the color is absent from the component, both coordinates are \\(D\\). Thus adjacent source labels have sup distance at most one.</p><p>Labels agree across an attachment edge, while each anchor edge has length at least the sup distance between its endpoint labels. Summing these inequalities along any path gives\n\\[\n\\|h(u)-h(v)\\|_\\infty\\le d_p(u,v).\n\\]\nThe own-color coordinate is zero by its definition, and anchor labels were copied from their assigned vertices. This proves the label assertions, including paths that use anchor shortcuts.</p><p>Every added edge has length at least \\(R=3L&gt;2L\\). A shortest path of length at most \\(2L\\) therefore uses only source edges. The closed radius-\\(2L\\) ball about an anchor is a singleton, and that about a source vertex is contained in its radius-\\(2L\\) ball in its own source graph.</p><p>In a \\(\\Delta\\)-regular multigraph, every vertex at graph distance at most \\(s\\) is reached by a walk of some length at most \\(s\\), and there are at most \\(\\Delta^t\\) walks of length \\(t\\) from a fixed vertex. Loops, parallel edges, and repeated vertices may cause several counted walks to reach the same vertex. Therefore every such metric ball has at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=0}^{2L}\\Delta^t\\le\\Delta^{2L+1}=Q\n\\]\npoints. The inequality follows from the geometric sum formula and \\(\\Delta\\ge2\\).</p><p>Finally, if \\(h_i(x)&lt;D\\) at a source vertex, its integer graph distance to a vertex of color \\(i\\) is at most \\(D-1\\). Witnesses for distinct colors are distinct vertices, so the number of such coordinates is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=0}^{D-1}\\Delta^t\\le\\Delta^{D+1}=H.\n\\]\nEvery anchor copies a source label, giving the same bound at all points of \\(M_p\\).</p><p>The source-to-basepoint lower bound also shows directly that the wedge of these blocks is unbounded: each block contains a source vertex whose distance from the common basepoint is at least \\(R_p=30p\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Section 4; the two constituent results are Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a finite set \\(S\\subseteq M_p\\) containing \\(o\\), a bounded finite-rank operator \\(T\\), and signed measures \\((\\mu_x)_{x\\in M_p}\\) on \\(S\\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p\\ge1\\), and use the constants \\(L,R,K,D,B,V\\) in (3.1).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((M_p,d_p,o)\\) be a countable pointed metric space with separation at least one and diameter at most \\(B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_p\\subseteq M_p\\) be finite and contain \\(o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h:M_p\\to\\{0,\\ldots,D\\}^k\\) be \\(1\\)-Lipschitz for the sup norm.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal V\\subseteq M_p\\) be a set of source vertices. Suppose that every \\(x\\in\\mathcal V\\) has an anchor \\(a(x)\\in A_p\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  h(a(x))=h(x),\n  \\qquad\n  d_p(x,a(x))\\le R.\n  \\]\n  These assumptions hold for the block in Proposition 4; they are also among the assumptions of the sufficient criterion in Remark 6.2 of the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that a bounded finite-rank linear operator\n  \\[\n  T_0:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\to\\mathcal F(M_p)\n  \\]\n  satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\|T_0\\|\\le p,\n  \\qquad\n  \\|T_0\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|&lt;\\frac12\n  \\quad(a\\in A_p).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(x\\in\\mathcal V\\), define\n  \\[\n  b_x(t)=\n  L\\left(1-\\frac{\\|h(x)-h(t)\\|_\\infty}{K}\\right)_+,\n  \\qquad s_+=\\max\\{s,0\\}. \\tag{4.2}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a signed measure \\(\\nu\\) on a finite set \\(S\\), write\n  \\[\n  |\\nu|(E)=\\sum_{j\\in E}|\\nu(\\{j\\})|,\n  \\qquad\n  \\|\\nu\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}=|\\nu|(S).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a finite set \\(S\\subseteq M_p\\) containing \\(o\\), a bounded finite-rank operator \\(T\\), and signed measures \\((\\mu_x)_{x\\in M_p}\\) on \\(S\\) such that\n\\[\nT\\mathcal F(M_p)\\subseteq\n\\operatorname{span}\\{\\delta_j:j\\in S\\},\n\\qquad\n\\|T\\|\\le2p,\n\\qquad\n\\|T\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|&lt;1\\quad(a\\in A_p), \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\neach \\(\\mu_x\\) has total mass one, and\n\\[\n\\int g\\,d\\mu_x\n=\n(T\\delta_x)(g-g(o))+g(o) \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nfor every real Lipschitz function \\(g\\) on \\(M_p\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\|\\mu_x\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\le V=1+4pB\n\\quad(x\\in M_p), \\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\int b_x\\,d\\mu_x\\ge\\frac{3L}{4}\n\\quad(x\\in\\mathcal V). \\tag{4.5}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 5.1 to obtain \\(T\\) and its common finite support \\(S\\). Lemma 5.2 then supplies the mass-one signed measures, their affine representation, the uniform total-variation bound, and the bump lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are a finite set \\(S\\subseteq M_p\\) containing \\(o\\) and a bounded finite-rank linear operator \\(T:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\to\\mathcal F(M_p)\\) satisfying (4.1).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the metric, anchor, and operator assumptions of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a finite set \\(S\\subseteq M_p\\) containing \\(o\\) and a bounded finite-rank linear operator \\(T:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\to\\mathcal F(M_p)\\) satisfying (4.1).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(T_0=0\\), take \\(T=0\\) and \\(S=\\{o\\}\\); the assumed anchor errors already give the required bounds.</p><p>Otherwise choose a basis \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_r\\) of the range of \\(T_0\\). The coordinate functionals on this finite-dimensional range are bounded. Composing them with \\(T_0\\) gives bounded functionals \\(\\varphi_i\\) on \\(\\mathcal F(M_p)\\) such that\n\\[\nT_0u=\\sum_{i=1}^r\\varphi_i(u)v_i.\n\\]\nChoose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\eta&lt;\\min\\{p,1/(2B)\\}.\n\\]\nFinite linear combinations of evaluations are dense in \\(\\mathcal F(M_p)\\), so choose such combinations \\(w_i\\) with\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^r\\|\\varphi_i\\|\\|w_i-v_i\\|&lt;\\eta.\n\\]\nThe operator\n\\[\nTu=\\sum_i\\varphi_i(u)w_i\n\\]\nthen satisfies \\(\\|T-T_0\\|&lt;\\eta\\).</p><p>Let \\(S\\) be the union of \\(\\{o\\}\\) and the finite supports of the chosen combinations \\(w_i\\). Its range has the asserted support, and\n\\[\n\\|T\\|\\le p+\\eta&lt;2p.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\|\\delta_a\\|=d_p(a,o)\\le B\\), for every \\(a\\in A_p\\),\n\\[\n\\|T\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|\n\\le\n\\|T_0\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|\n+\\|T-T_0\\|\\|\\delta_a\\|\n&lt;\n\\frac12+\\eta B&lt;1.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a family \\((\\mu_x)_{x\\in M_p}\\) of signed measures on \\(S\\), each of total mass one, satisfying (4.3), (4.4), and (4.5).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the metric, label, source-vertex, anchor, and bump assumptions of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Instead of assuming an operator \\(T_0\\), assume directly that \\(S\\subseteq M_p\\) is finite and contains \\(o\\), and that a bounded linear operator \\(T:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\to\\mathcal F(M_p)\\) satisfies (4.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a family \\((\\mu_x)_{x\\in M_p}\\) of signed measures on \\(S\\), each of total mass one, satisfying (4.3), (4.4), and (4.5).</p>", "proof": "<p>For each \\(x\\), express\n\\[\nT\\delta_x=\\sum_{j\\in S\\setminus\\{o\\}}c_x(j)\\delta_j\n\\]\nand set\n\\[\n\\mu_x(\\{j\\})=c_x(j)\\quad(j\\ne o),\n\\qquad\n\\mu_x(\\{o\\})=\n1-\\sum_{j\\in S\\setminus\\{o\\}}c_x(j).\n\\]\nThe empty sum is zero when \\(S=\\{o\\}\\). These masses sum to one, and\n\\[\n\\int g\\,d\\mu_x\n=\n\\sum_{j\\in S\\setminus\\{o\\}}\nc_x(j)\\bigl(g(j)-g(o)\\bigr)+g(o),\n\\]\nwhich is (4.3).</p><p>To estimate the total variation, let \\(g\\) take the sign of \\(\\mu_x(\\{j\\})\\) at each \\(j\\in S\\) and be zero off \\(S\\), using \\(\\operatorname{sign}(0)=0\\). Its values lie in \\([-1,1]\\), so separation by one gives \\(\\|g\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}\\le2\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mu_x\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n&amp;=\\int g\\,d\\mu_x\\\\\n&amp;=(T\\delta_x)(g-g(o))+g(o)\\\\\n&amp;\\le2\\|T\\delta_x\\|+1\\\\\n&amp;\\le1+4pB.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last inequality uses \\(\\|T\\|\\le2p\\) and \\(\\|\\delta_x\\|=d_p(x,o)\\le B\\). In particular, the estimate includes the mass at the basepoint.</p><p>The bump \\(b_x\\) takes values in \\([0,L]\\), has Lipschitz constant at most \\(L/K\\le1\\), and satisfies \\(b_x(a(x))=L\\). For a source vertex \\(x\\), the anchor assumptions and (4.1) imply\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|T\\delta_x-\\delta_{a(x)}\\|\n&amp;\\le\n\\|T(\\delta_x-\\delta_{a(x)})\\|\n+\\|T\\delta_{a(x)}-\\delta_{a(x)}\\|\\\\\n&amp;\\le2pR+1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(b_x(a(x))=L\\), the affine pairing gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int b_x\\,d\\mu_x-L\n&amp;=(T\\delta_x-\\delta_{a(x)})\n   (b_x-b_x(o))\\\\\n&amp;\\ge-\\frac{L}{K}(2pR+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\frac{2pR+1}{K}\\le\\frac15&lt;\\frac14,\n\\]\nbecause \\(K=10p(R+1)\\) and \\(p\\ge1\\). This proves (4.5).</p><p>The measures may have coefficients of either sign. Their common total mass means that \\(\\mu_x-\\mu_y\\) annihilates constants, so centering a scalar test at \\(o\\) does not change its pairing with this difference.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Section 5; the constituent results are Lemmas 5.1 and 5.3, with the optional enlargement in Remark 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One can choose \\(n\\ge2\\) and then \\(m\\) so that the fraction \\(\\beta\\) of bad vertices in \\(G_m\\) satisfies \\( \\beta<\\frac1{20V}. \\) For these choices:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p\\ge1\\), with \\(L,R,K,D,B,V,\\Delta,Q,H,k\\) chosen as in Proposition 4. In particular,\n  \\[\n  K+2L+1=D-4&lt;D,\n  \\qquad\n  \\frac{\\Delta QH}{k}&lt;\\frac12.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((M_p,d_p,o)\\) be a metric space carrying a label map\n  \\[\n  h:M_p\\to\\{0,\\ldots,D\\}^k.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that every closed \\(d_p\\)-ball of radius \\(2L\\) has at most \\(Q\\) points and every label \\(h(t)\\) has at most \\(H\\) coordinates strictly below \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G_m\\) be nonempty finite \\(\\Delta\\)-regular multigraphs with disjoint vertex sets in \\(M_p\\), colored by \\(\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}\\), satisfying the colored local convergence in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that a graph vertex \\(x\\) of color \\(i\\) satisfies \\(h_i(x)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite state set \\(S\\subseteq M_p\\) containing \\(o\\). Its labels and metric distances are fixed throughout the argument.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(e:x\\to y\\) for an oriented incidence and \\(D(x)\\) for the set of \\(\\Delta\\) outgoing incidences at \\(x\\). A walk is nonbacktracking if it never immediately takes the reverse incidence of its preceding step. Vertices and states may repeat.</p><p class=\"li\">A graph vertex \\(x\\) and a state \\(j\\in S\\) are compatible when\n  \\[\n  \\|h(x)-h(j)\\|_\\infty\\le K+2L+1=D-4.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A nonbacktracking walk\n  \\[\n  x_0,e_1,x_1,\\ldots,e_t,x_t\n  \\]\n  in one graph is matched if there are states \\(j_0,\\ldots,j_t\\in S\\) such that every \\((x_s,j_s)\\) is compatible and\n  \\[\n  d_p(j_{s-1},j_s)\\le2L\n  \\quad(1\\le s\\le t).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For fixed \\(n\\ge2\\) and \\(G=G_m\\), a vertex is bad if a matched walk of length \\(n\\) starts there, and good otherwise.</p><p class=\"li\">At a good vertex \\(x\\), for \\(e\\in D(x)\\) and \\(j\\in S\\), let \\(\\mathcal L_x(e,j)\\) be the set of positive lengths of matched walks starting through \\(e\\) with initial state \\(j\\), and set\n  \\[\n  r_x(e,j)=\\max\\bigl(\\{0\\}\\cup\\mathcal L_x(e,j)\\bigr).\n  \\]\n  Declare \\(e\\) exceptional at \\((x,j)\\) if its record is strictly greater than every other outgoing record at \\((x,j)\\). At a bad vertex, declare all outgoing incidences exceptional at every state. Put\n  \\[\n  E_e=\\{j\\in S:e\\text{ is exceptional at }(x,j)\\}\n  \\quad(e:x\\to y).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can choose \\(n\\ge2\\) and then \\(m\\) so that the fraction \\(\\beta\\) of bad vertices in \\(G_m\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\beta&lt;\\frac1{20V}.\n\\]\nFor these choices:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. At every good vertex, each state belongs to at most one exceptional set.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(e:x\\to y\\), both \\((x,j)\\) and \\((y,j')\\) are compatible, and \\(d_p(j,j')\\le2L\\), then\n   \\[\n   j\\in E_e\\quad\\text{or}\\quad j'\\in E_{\\bar e}.\n   \\]</p><p>The exceptional-set conclusion holds for every fixed \\(n\\ge2\\) and every graph \\(G_m\\), independently of the smallness of its bad-vertex fraction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 6.1 supplies \\(n\\ge2\\) and then \\(m\\) with bad-vertex fraction less than \\(1/(20V)\\). At a good vertex, strict maximality of an outgoing record permits at most one exceptional incidence per state. Lemma 6.2 gives the required exceptional-set conclusion for compatible states at the ends of an incidence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1; the additional observation is Remark 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\limsup_{m\\to\\infty}\\beta_m \\le |S|\\frac Hk \\left(\\frac{\\Delta QH}{k}\\right)^n. \\) Consequently, one can choose \\(n\\ge2\\) and then \\(m\\) so that \\( \\beta_m<\\frac1{20V}. \\) The same bound and conclusion remain valid …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the state, label, graph, compatibility, and matching assumptions of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">For a fixed integer \\(n\\ge1\\), let \\(\\beta_m\\) be the fraction of vertices of \\(G_m\\) from which a matched walk of length \\(n\\) starts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\limsup_{m\\to\\infty}\\beta_m\n\\le\n|S|\\frac Hk\n\\left(\\frac{\\Delta QH}{k}\\right)^n. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nConsequently, one can choose \\(n\\ge2\\) and then \\(m\\) so that\n\\[\n\\beta_m&lt;\\frac1{20V}.\n\\]</p><p>The same bound and conclusion remain valid if the counted roots are enlarged to include every root whose induced radius-\\((n+1)\\) neighborhood is not a tree.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(j\\in S\\), set\n\\[\nC_j=\\{i:h_i(j)&lt;D\\}.\n\\]\nThe label-coordinate bound gives \\(|C_j|\\le H\\). If \\(x\\) has color \\(i\\) and is compatible with \\(j\\), then \\(h_i(x)=0\\) implies\n\\[\nh_i(j)\\le K+2L+1=D-4&lt;D.\n\\]\nThus the color of \\(x\\) belongs to \\(C_j\\). This is only a necessary condition for compatibility; it avoids any independence assertion about the full labels.</p><p>Consider the weaker event that a nonbacktracking length-\\(n\\) walk\n\\[\nx_0,e_1,x_1,\\ldots,e_n,x_n\n\\]\nfrom the root has states \\(j_0,\\ldots,j_n\\in S\\) with\n\\[\nd_p(j_{s-1},j_s)\\le2L\\quad(1\\le s\\le n)\n\\]\nand with the color of \\(x_s\\) in \\(C_{j_s}\\) for \\(0\\le s\\le n\\).</p><p>Its occurrence is determined by the induced colored radius-\\(n\\) ball: every traversed vertex and edge lies in that ball, including loops and parallel edge instances. There are only finitely many such rooted types. With \\(S\\) and \\(n\\) fixed, the sets \\(C_j\\) and the transition relation are fixed, so the event selects the same finite union of these types for every \\(m\\).</p><p>The assumed local convergence gives convergence for every type and therefore for this finite union. Its empirical frequency converges to its probability on the independently colored \\(\\Delta\\)-regular tree. The support may have been chosen using the entire deterministic graph sequence: once fixed, it only selects the types in the union. No random colors are conditioned on the support.</p><p>On the tree there are at most \\(\\Delta^n\\) nonbacktracking walks of length \\(n\\) from the root, each visiting \\(n+1\\) distinct vertices. For each state \\(j\\), its allowed successors lie in\n\\[\nS\\cap\\overline B_{d_p}(j,2L),\n\\]\nwhich has at most \\(Q\\) points. Thus there are at most \\(|S|Q^n\\) allowed state sequences.</p><p>For a fixed walk and state sequence, independence of the colors gives probability at most \\((H/k)^{n+1}\\) for all required memberships. Repeated states and overlapping sets \\(C_j\\) do not affect this product, because the source vertices on the tree are distinct. A union bound gives\n\\[\n\\Delta^n|S|Q^n(H/k)^{n+1}\n=\n|S|(H/k)(\\Delta QH/k)^n.\n\\]\nEvery matched walk satisfies the weaker event, proving (5.1).</p><p>Since \\(k&gt;2\\Delta QH\\), the factor \\(\\Delta QH/k\\) is less than \\(1/2\\). Choose \\(n\\ge2\\) so that the right-hand side of (5.1) is less than \\(1/(40V)\\), and then choose \\(m\\) with \\(\\beta_m&lt;1/(20V)\\).</p><p>For the optional enlargement, let \\(\\gamma_m\\) be the fraction of roots whose induced radius-\\((n+1)\\) neighborhood is not a tree, and let \\(\\widetilde\\beta_m\\) be the enlarged fraction. Then\n\\[\n\\beta_m\\le\\widetilde\\beta_m\\le\\beta_m+\\gamma_m,\n\\]\nwhile the local limit gives \\(\\gamma_m\\to0\\). Thus (5.1) remains valid with \\(\\widetilde\\beta_m\\) in place of \\(\\beta_m\\), and the same successive choices of \\(n\\) and sufficiently large \\(m\\) give \\(\\widetilde\\beta_m&lt;1/(20V)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\((x,j)\\) and \\((y,j')\\) are compatible and \\(d_p(j,j')\\le2L\\), then \\( j\\in E_e\\quad\\text{or}\\quad j'\\in E_{\\bar e}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the compatibility, matching, incidence, and exceptional-set definitions of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix any \\(n\\ge2\\) and any \\(G=G_m\\); no bound on the bad-vertex fraction is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(e:x\\to y\\) be an incidence of \\(G\\), and let \\(j,j'\\in S\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\((x,j)\\) and \\((y,j')\\) are compatible and \\(d_p(j,j')\\le2L\\), then\n\\[\nj\\in E_e\\quad\\text{or}\\quad j'\\in E_{\\bar e}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At a good vertex, every matched walk has length less than \\(n\\), since a matched walk of length at least \\(n\\) has a matched prefix of length \\(n\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_x(e,j)\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-1\\},\n\\]\nand the records are well-defined. The value zero means only that no positive-length walk exists; it makes no assertion about compatibility of \\((x,j)\\).</p><p>If either endpoint of \\(e\\) is bad, the conclusion follows from the definition. Suppose both are good, and put\n\\[\nA_x=\\max_{g\\in D(x)\\setminus\\{e\\}}r_x(g,j),\n\\qquad\nA_y=\\max_{f\\in D(y)\\setminus\\{\\bar e\\}}r_y(f,j').\n\\]\nThe maxima are over nonempty finite sets, since \\(\\Delta\\ge4\\).</p><p>If \\(A_y&gt;0\\), choose a walk realizing it and prepend \\(e\\), with the state transition \\(j\\) to \\(j'\\). The old first incidence is not \\(\\bar e\\), so the resulting walk is nonbacktracking and matched. If \\(A_y=0\\), the edge alone, with states \\(j,j'\\), is a matched walk.</p><p>The same argument after reversing \\(e\\) therefore gives both inequalities\n\\[\nr_x(e,j)\\ge1+A_y,\n\\qquad\nr_y(\\bar e,j')\\ge1+A_x. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nIf neither incidence were exceptional, their records would be at most \\(A_x\\) and \\(A_y\\), respectively. These inequalities would force\n\\[\nA_x\\ge1+A_y\\ge2+A_x,\n\\]\na contradiction.</p><p>The proof uses incidence identities, so it also applies to loops and parallel edges. It allows repeated states and vertex revisits, and using the edge by itself covers zero records.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 1.2, the finite-anchor obstruction; and Remark 6.2, its sufficient criterion", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every bounded finite-rank real linear operator \\( T_0:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F(M_p), \\qquad \\|T_0\\|\\le p, \\) satisfies \\( \\max_{a\\in A_p}\\|T_0\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|\\ge\\frac12. \\) In particular, Proposition …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer \\(p\\ge1\\), and retain exactly the parameter choices\n  \\[\n  L=10p,\\quad R=30p,\\quad K=10p(R+1),\\quad\n  D=K+2L+5,\\quad B=4R+D,\\quad V=1+4pB,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\Delta\\ge4\\text{ even},\\quad\n  V/\\Delta&lt;1/20,\\quad\n  Q=\\Delta^{2L+1},\\quad H=\\Delta^{D+1},\\quad\n  k&gt;2\\Delta QH.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((M_p,d_p,o)\\) be a countable pointed metric space with separation at least one and diameter at most \\(B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_p\\subseteq M_p\\) be finite and contain \\(o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G_m\\) be nonempty finite \\(\\Delta\\)-regular multigraphs with disjoint vertex sets in \\(M_p\\), colored by \\(\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}\\). Use the incidence convention of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that source-edge endpoints have \\(d_p\\)-distance at most one and that the empirical distributions of induced rooted colored balls converge to those of the \\(\\Delta\\)-regular tree with independent uniform colors, as in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that\n  \\[\n  h:M_p\\to\\{0,\\ldots,D\\}^k\n  \\]\n  is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz for the sup norm. Every source vertex \\(x\\) of color \\(i\\) is required to satisfy \\(h_i(x)=0\\) and to have an anchor \\(a(x)\\in A_p\\) with\n  \\[\n  h(a(x))=h(x),\n  \\qquad d_p(x,a(x))\\le R.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that every closed \\(d_p\\)-ball of radius \\(2L\\) has at most \\(Q\\) points and every \\(h(t)\\) has at most \\(H\\) coordinates strictly below \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All these data are fixed before the operator below is chosen.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every bounded finite-rank real linear operator\n\\[\nT_0:\\mathcal F(M_p)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F(M_p),\n\\qquad \\|T_0\\|\\le p,\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\max_{a\\in A_p}\\|T_0\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|\\ge\\frac12.\n\\]</p><p>In particular, Proposition 4 supplies, for each integer \\(p\\ge1\\), a bounded countably infinite pointed metric space and a finite anchor set satisfying this obstruction and\n\\[\nd_p(x,y)\\ge1\\quad(x\\ne y),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{diam}M_p\\le\nB_p=300p^2+150p+5.\n\\]\nThis is the block assertion of original Theorem 1.2.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose that a bounded finite-rank operator \\(T_0\\) of norm at most \\(p\\) has error less than \\(1/2\\) on every anchor.</p><p>Proposition 5 supplies \\(T,S\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\|T\\|\\le2p,\n\\qquad\nT\\mathcal F(M_p)\\subseteq\n\\operatorname{span}\\{\\delta_j:j\\in S\\},\n\\qquad\n\\|T\\delta_a-\\delta_a\\|&lt;1\\quad(a\\in A_p),\n\\]\nand mass-one signed measures \\(\\mu_x\\) on \\(S\\) such that\n\\[\n\\int g\\,d\\mu_x=(T\\delta_x)(g-g(o))+g(o),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\mu_x\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\le V,\n\\qquad\n\\int b_x\\,d\\mu_x\\ge\\frac{3L}{4}\n\\]\nfor every source vertex \\(x\\).</p><p>With this state set fixed, Proposition 6 gives \\(n\\ge2\\) and then \\(G=G_m\\) so that the fraction \\(\\beta\\) of bad vertices is less than \\(1/(20V)\\). Apply Lemma 7.1 to these choices.</p><p>For an incidence \\(e:x\\to y\\), the test prescriptions imply\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int f_e\\,d(\\mu_x-\\mu_y)\n&amp;\\ge\n\\int b_x\\,d\\mu_x+\\int b_y\\,d\\mu_y\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\n-2L\\bigl(|\\mu_x|(E_e)+|\\mu_y|(E_{\\bar e})\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\n\\frac{3L}{2}\n-2L\\bigl(|\\mu_x|(E_e)+|\\mu_y|(E_{\\bar e})\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned} \\qquad(6.1)\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(f_e-b_x\\) vanishes on \\(S\\setminus E_e\\) and has absolute value at most \\(2L\\) elsewhere; the same statement holds for \\(f_e+b_y\\) and \\(E_{\\bar e}\\). Integration against the variation measures bounds both errors, regardless of the signs of the coefficients.</p><p>On the other hand, the affine pairing gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\int f_e\\,d(\\mu_x-\\mu_y)\\right|\n=\n\\left|\n(T(\\delta_x-\\delta_y))(f_e-f_e(o))\n\\right|\n\\le2p, \\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nbecause\n\\[\n\\|T\\|\\le2p,\\qquad\n\\|f_e\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}\\le1,\\qquad\n\\|\\delta_x-\\delta_y\\|=d_p(x,y)\\le1.\n\\]\nFor a loop, \\(x=y\\) and the left side is zero.</p><p>Choose a uniform vertex \\(x\\) of \\(G\\) and then a uniform incidence \\(e\\in D(x)\\). At a good vertex, each fixed state occurs in at most one exceptional set. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\frac1\\Delta\\sum_{e\\in D(x)}|\\mu_x|(E_e)\n\\le\n\\frac1\\Delta\\sum_{j\\in S}|\\mu_x(\\{j\\})|\n\\le\\frac V\\Delta.\n\\]\nAt a bad vertex the average is at most \\(V\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\bigl[|\\mu_x|(E_e)\\bigr]\n\\le\n\\frac V\\Delta+V\\beta\n&lt;\\frac1{10}. \\tag{6.3}\n\\]</p><p>The \\(\\Delta\\)-regularity of \\(G\\) makes this sampling uniform on all oriented incidences. Reversal preserves that uniform distribution, exchanging the two incidences of a loop as well. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\bigl[|\\mu_y|(E_{\\bar e})\\bigr]&lt;\\frac1{10}\n\\]\ntoo.</p><p>Taking expectations in (6.1) and using (6.2) forces\n\\[\n2p&gt;\n\\frac{3L}{2}-\\frac{4L}{10}\n=\n\\frac{11L}{10}\n=\n11p,\n\\]\na contradiction.</p><p>The argument uses only the assumptions explicitly listed in this proposition: the support perturbation and signed-measure estimates use the metric, anchor, and label bounds, while the rare-walk, continuation, scalar-test, and averaging steps use those bounds, the stated graph limit, and the incidence convention. This proves the sufficient criterion of original Remark 6.2.</p><p>For the blocks constructed in Proposition 4, all these assumptions hold. The construction fixed \\(p\\), all constants, the entire graph sequence, \\(M_p\\), and \\(A_p\\) before \\(T_0\\) was considered. Only after \\(T_0\\) produced the finite support \\(S\\) were \\(n\\) and then \\(m\\) chosen. Thus the same finite anchor set defeats every operator in the stated class, proving original Theorem 1.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every incidence \\(e:x\\to y\\) of \\(G\\), there is a \\(1\\)-Lipschitz function \\( f_e:M_p\\longrightarrow[-L,L] \\) such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the metric, label, source-edge, and parameter assumptions of Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\subseteq M_p\\) be finite and contain \\(o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(n\\ge2\\) and \\(G=G_m\\). Define compatibility, matched walks, good and bad vertices, continuation records, and exceptional sets \\(E_e\\) exactly as in Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">For each source vertex \\(x\\), use the bump\n  \\[\n  b_x(t)=\n  L\\left(1-\\frac{\\|h(x)-h(t)\\|_\\infty}{K}\\right)_+.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every incidence \\(e:x\\to y\\) of \\(G\\), there is a \\(1\\)-Lipschitz function\n\\[\nf_e:M_p\\longrightarrow[-L,L]\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\nf_e(j)=b_x(j)\\quad(j\\in S\\setminus E_e),\n\\qquad\nf_e(j')=-b_y(j')\\quad(j'\\in S\\setminus E_{\\bar e}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Within either prescription set, the Lipschitz inequality follows from the bump bound. Across the two sets, the difference between the prescribed values at \\(j,j'\\) is\n\\[\nb_x(j)+b_y(j')\\le2L.\n\\]\nThus the inequality holds whenever \\(d_p(j,j')\\ge2L\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(d_p(j,j')&lt;2L\\) and \\(b_x(j)&gt;0\\). Then\n\\[\n\\|h(x)-h(j)\\|_\\infty&lt;K.\n\\]\nSince \\(d_p(x,y)\\le1\\) and \\(h\\) is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|h(y)-h(j')\\|_\\infty\n&amp;\\le\n\\|h(y)-h(x)\\|_\\infty\n+\\|h(x)-h(j)\\|_\\infty\n+\\|h(j)-h(j')\\|_\\infty\\\\\n&amp;&lt;1+K+2L.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe pairs \\((x,j)\\) and \\((y,j')\\) are compatible, and their state distance is at most \\(2L\\). By the exceptional-set conclusion of Proposition 6, both prescriptions cannot be present.</p><p>The same reasoning applies when \\(b_y(j')&gt;0\\). Hence, whenever two states, one from each prescription set, are less than \\(2L\\) apart, both prescribed values are zero. This proves the remaining Lipschitz inequalities and, by taking \\(j=j'\\), consistency on the intersection of the two prescription sets.</p><p>Let \\(P\\subseteq S\\) be their union and let \\(v:P\\to[-L,L]\\) be the resulting \\(1\\)-Lipschitz data. If \\(P\\) is empty, take \\(f_e=0\\). Otherwise use the scalar Lipschitz extension formula, followed by clipping:\n\\[\nF(z)=\\min_{j\\in P}\\{v(j)+d_p(z,j)\\},\n\\qquad\nf_e(z)=\\max\\{-L,\\min\\{L,F(z)\\}\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\in P\\), the Lipschitz inequalities for \\(v\\) give \\(F(z)=v(z)\\). The triangle inequality gives\n\\[\nF(z)\\le F(w)+d_p(z,w)\n\\]\nand the same inequality with \\(z,w\\) exchanged. Thus \\(F\\) is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz. Clipping to \\([-L,L]\\) preserves both the Lipschitz bound and the prescribed values.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 10, "cite"], [5, 13, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 2, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [6, 13, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [10, 14, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 2, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"]]});
